    <!-- ================================================================ -->
    <!-- 4. RECONSTRUCTION, VALIDATION, AND FEATURES ≈ 1500-1800 words.   -->
    <!-- ================================================================ -->

    <section xml:id="sec-method">
      <title>Reconstruction, Validation, and Feature Construction</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          The processing pipeline preserves three distinct layers: immutable raw
          rows, reconstructed states and attempts, and derived features.
          Reconstruction is performed before scoring or process analysis, and each
          derived object retains provenance identifying the raw import, item
          source, software stage, and configuration that produced it.
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <subsection xml:id="subsec-order-segmentation">
        <title>Event Ordering and Attempt Segmentation</title>

        <p>
          Events are grouped by learner and item and ordered deterministically
          using parsed time, problem-view information, and stable file order as a
          final tie breaker.  Ties that remain unresolved by substantive ordering
          fields are retained as a quality-control flag rather than silently
          treated as certain.
        </p>

        <p>
          The current implementation defines an attempt as one learner's work on
          one item within a sitting.  The default segmentation separates attempts
          at session changes, page views, or inactivity gaps exceeding 30 minutes;
          a reset restores the initial state within the attempt rather than
          automatically starting a new attempt.  The segmentation rule is stored
          with the derived attempt and is treated as a sensitivity parameter in
          this study.
        </p>

        <!--
        SEGMENTATION COMPLETION CHECKLIST
        1. Produce distributions of within-trace gaps, session changes, page views, and resets.
        2. Inspect traces around candidate 10, 20, 30, and 60 minute boundaries.
        3. Freeze one primary segmentation rule before computing headline results.
        4. Decide whether events after a correct check remain in the same analytic attempt.
        5. Decide whether repeated page rows create boundaries or only delimit access.
        6. Record the exact rule in the pipeline run manifest.
        7. Implement at least two plausible alternatives for RQ4.
        8. Report how many attempts each boundary type creates.
        -->
      </subsection>

      <subsection xml:id="subsec-replay">
        <title>State Replay</title>

        <p>
          Replay initializes the board at <m>s_0=(0,\ldots,0)</m>.  For each
          decoded move, the source zone is read from the current reconstructed
          state and the card coordinate is replaced by the decoded destination.
          A reset restores <m>s_0</m>.  A check repeats the current reconstructed
          state and stores it beside the full checked configuration decoded from
          the row's <c>Input</c> payload.  Page events contribute ordering and
          boundary information but do not update the board.
        </p>

        <p>
          A malformed or off-board event is never silently repaired.  The raw
          event remains traceable, the replay decision is recorded, and an
          appropriate parse or validity flag is propagated to the enclosing
          attempt.  No-op drops are retained and flagged but are excluded from
          effective-move counts by default.
        </p>

        <table xml:id="tab-replay-rules">
          <title>Canonical replay rules.</title>
          <tabular valign="top">
            <col width="10%" />
            <col width="34%" />
            <col width="56%" />
            <row header="yes">
              <cell><p>Event</p></cell>
              <cell><p>Payload</p></cell>
              <cell><p>Replay effect</p></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell><p>move</p></cell>
              <cell><p>card <m>j</m>, destination <m>h</m></p></cell>
              <cell><p>Set <m>s(j)\leftarrow h</m>; flag no-op or invalid labels when applicable.</p></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell><p>check</p></cell>
              <cell><p>full submitted configuration and feedback</p></cell>
              <cell><p>Repeat the replayed state and compare it with the independently decoded check state.</p></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell><p>reset</p></cell>
              <cell><p>reset mark</p></cell>
              <cell><p>Set <m>s\leftarrow s_0</m>.</p></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell><p>page</p></cell>
              <cell><p>access and ordering fields</p></cell>
              <cell><p>No state change; retain for segmentation and provenance.</p></cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>
      </subsection>

      <subsection xml:id="subsec-anchor-validation">
        <title>Check-Anchor Validation</title>

        <p>
          Check rows provide the strongest available internal validation because
          they contain a complete state that is decoded independently of the
          preceding move sequence.  An anchor is eligible when its <c>Input</c>
          payload can be parsed and its labels align with the item specification.
          Exact anchor agreement occurs when the replayed state immediately before
          the check equals the decoded checked state.
        </p>

        <p>
          Agreement is reported at the check and attempt levels.  Noncomparability
          and disagreement are separated into missing or malformed payloads,
          unknown labels, invalid checked states, source-map problems, ambiguous
          ordering, skipped events, and unexplained mismatches.  A mismatch is
          treated as evidence that the reconstructed trajectory cannot be assumed
          complete.
        </p>

        <!--
        VALIDATION COMPLETION CHECKLIST
        1. Define the denominator for eligible anchors before calculating agreement.
        2. Export one row per check with replayed state, decoded state, eligibility,
           exact-match indicator, mismatch distance, and diagnostic flags.
        3. Calculate overall and item-specific agreement with uncertainty intervals.
        4. Classify every nonmatching or noncomparable check into a prespecified cause.
        5. Draw a stratified manual-audit sample containing matches, mismatches,
           limited attempts, excluded attempts, every item, and every offering.
        6. Write a reviewer protocol that reconstructs the expected state without
           consulting the pipeline's match label.
        7. Use two reviewers for a subset if the audit involves interpretive coding.
        8. Add synthetic fault cases for a missing move, swapped row order,
           duplicate event, malformed JSON, unknown card, unknown zone, stale source,
           and invalid double placement.
        9. Verify that each synthetic fault produces the intended flag or mismatch.
        10. Summarize automated, manual, synthetic, and regression-test evidence separately.
        -->
      </subsection>

      <subsection xml:id="subsec-qc-readiness">
        <title>Quality Control and Feature Readiness</title>

        <p>
          Quality-control flags are stored per attempt rather than resolved through
          undocumented cleaning.  The implemented vocabulary includes successful
          replay, check mismatch, parse error, missing source map, ambiguous row
          order, invalid state, no-op move, duplicate row, session break, and long
          idle gap.  Readiness is computed from these flags rather than from
          student performance.
        </p>

        <p>
          An attempt is <em>ready</em> when a source map is available, parsing and
          state validity succeed, every comparable check agrees with replay, and
          no warning remains beyond an informational no-op.  It is
          <em>excluded</em> when the source map required for key-based features is
          absent or when an invalid state makes checked-response features
          unreliable.  All other attempts are <em>limited</em>: some features may
          remain usable, but the warning must be respected by the analysis.
        </p>

        <table xml:id="tab-readiness-policy">
          <title>Feature-readiness policy.</title>
          <tabular valign="top">
            <col width="20%" />
            <col width="40%" />
            <col width="40%" />
            <row header="yes">
              <cell><p>Status</p></cell>
              <cell><p>Operational condition</p></cell>
              <cell><p>Primary analytic use</p></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell><p>ready</p></cell>
              <cell><p>Anchor-validated replay with no substantive warning.</p></cell>
              <cell><p>All prespecified features and primary analyses.</p></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell><p>limited</p></cell>
              <cell><p>Replay not fully anchor-verified or warning affects selected features.</p></cell>
              <cell><p>Only analyses unaffected by the specific flag; included in sensitivity analyses when justified.</p></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell><p>exclude</p></cell>
              <cell><p>Missing source map or invalid state prevents defensible key-based or checked-state features.</p></cell>
              <cell><p>QC accounting only.</p></cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>

        <!--
        READINESS COMPLETION CHECKLIST
        1. Freeze the exact flag vocabulary and readiness function used for the paper.
        2. Generate a flag-by-feature availability matrix.
        3. Verify that readiness never depends on correctness, score, or apparent difficulty.
        4. Count attempts carrying each individual flag and each common flag combination.
        5. Inspect whether readiness varies by item, offering, or source version.
        6. Prespecify the primary analysis as ready attempts unless a feature-specific
           rule justifies otherwise.
        7. Use ready plus limited only as a named sensitivity condition.
        8. Move the complete rulebook to supplementary material if the main table becomes too large.
        -->
      </subsection>

      <subsection xml:id="subsec-baseline-features">
        <title>Baseline Outcome and Process Features</title>

        <p>
          Features are computed only after reconstruction status is known.  The
          primary state feature is key distance <m>d^\star(X)</m>.  Attempt
          outcomes include first-check distance, final-check distance,
          first-check correctness, eventual correctness, and the ordinal of the
          first correct check.  Error profiles distinguish cards left in the tray
          when placement is required from cards assigned to an incorrect slot;
          decoy-card and decoy-slot behavior is retained when defined by the item.
        </p>

        <p>
          Process features include effective moves, no-op moves, takebacks,
          resets, unique cards moved, repeated moves, number and timing of checks,
          moves before the first check, post-check gain, wall-clock duration, and
          active duration after removing gaps beyond a reported idle threshold.
          Excess movement compares the observed number of effective moves with a
          shortest-path benchmark.  These features describe observable behavior;
          none uniquely identifies a cognitive strategy.
        </p>

        <table xml:id="tab-primary-features">
          <title>Planned primary feature set for Paper 1.</title>
          <tabular valign="top">
            <col width="20%" />
            <col width="40%" />
            <col width="40%" />
            <row header="yes">
              <cell><p>Family</p></cell>
              <cell><p>Primary fields</p></cell>
              <cell><p>Role</p></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell><p>Endpoint</p></cell>
              <cell><p>first and final check distance; first-check and eventual correctness; correct-check ordinal</p></cell>
              <cell><p>Define outcome-equivalent groups and summarize success.</p></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell><p>Error profile</p></cell>
              <cell><p>omissions; wrong-slot placements; decoy-card and decoy-slot errors</p></cell>
              <cell><p>Describe how checked states differ from the key.</p></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell><p>Movement</p></cell>
              <cell><p>effective moves; no-ops; takebacks; resets; repeated cards; excess movement</p></cell>
              <cell><p>Describe path length, revision, and efficiency.</p></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell><p>Checking and time</p></cell>
              <cell><p>number of checks; moves to first check; post-check gain; active and elapsed duration</p></cell>
              <cell><p>Describe checking cadence and temporal structure.</p></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell><p>Quality</p></cell>
              <cell><p>QC flags; readiness; anchor coverage</p></cell>
              <cell><p>Control analytic eligibility and sensitivity analyses.</p></cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>

